::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q01::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L02_Q01
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Which number is greatest?
Number line reference: -9 ... -2 ... 0 ... 4. Values farther right are greater.
{
=A. 4#Correct. Positive 4 is to the right of the negative values and zero.
~B. -9#This is the farthest left value, so it is the least, not the greatest.
~C. -2#This is greater than -9 but still less than any positive number.
~D. 0#Zero is greater than negative numbers but less than 4.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q02::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L02_Q02
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Which comparison is true?
Number line reference: -7 ... -2 ... 0. Values farther left are less.
{
~A. -2 < -7#This reverses the order. -2 is farther right, so it is greater than -7.
~B. -7 > 0#A negative number is less than zero.
=C. -7 < -2#Correct. -7 is farther left on the number line than -2.
~D. 0 < -2#Zero is greater than any negative number.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q03::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L02_Q03
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
What is the opposite of -11?
Number line reference: -11 and 11 are the same distance from 0 on opposite sides.
{
~A. -11#This is the original number, not its opposite.
=B. 11#Correct. Opposites are the same distance from zero on opposite sides of the number line.
~C. 0#Zero is not the opposite of -11.
~D. -1/11#This is a reciprocal-like error, not the opposite.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q04::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L02_Q04
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Evaluate |-13|.
Distance reference: -13 is 13 units from 0, and absolute value measures distance.
{
~A. -13#Absolute value is distance from zero, so it cannot be negative.
~B. 0#The number is not zero units from zero.
~C. 26#This doubles the distance instead of finding the distance from zero.
=D. 13#Correct. -13 is 13 units from zero.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q05::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L02_Q05
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Order from least to greatest: -3, 5, -8, 0.
Number line reference from left to right: -8 ... -3 ... 0 ... 5.
{
=A. -8, -3, 0, 5#Correct. From left to right on the number line, the order is -8, -3, 0, 5.
~B. -3, -8, 0, 5#This puts -3 before -8, but -8 is farther left and is smaller.
~C. 5, 0, -3, -8#This orders from greatest to least.
~D. -8, 0, -3, 5#This places 0 before -3, but 0 is greater than -3.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q06::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L02_Q06
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Evaluate -9 + 4.
Movement reference: start at -9, move 4 units right, and keep the result on the negative side.
{
~A. -13#This adds the absolute values and keeps a negative sign. The signs are different, so subtract the absolute values.
=B. -5#Correct. The signs are different, so 9 - 4 = 5, and the negative number has the larger absolute value.
~C. 5#This uses the correct distance but the wrong sign.
~D. 13#This adds absolute values and uses a positive sign.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q07::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L02_Q07
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Evaluate -6 + (-8).
Sign reference: adding two negatives means move left and keep the negative sign.
{
~A. -2#This subtracts the absolute values, but the signs are the same.
~B. 2#This subtracts and uses a positive sign, which does not match adding two negatives.
~C. 14#This adds the absolute values but uses the wrong sign.
=D. -14#Correct. Same signs mean add the absolute values and keep the negative sign.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q08::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L02_Q08
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Evaluate 12 + (-15).
Movement reference: start at 12, move 15 units left, and end 3 units left of 0.
{
~A. -27#This adds the absolute values and keeps a negative sign. Different signs require subtraction.
~B. 3#This subtracts correctly but uses the wrong sign.
~C. 27#This adds the absolute values and uses a positive sign.
=D. -3#Correct. The signs are different, so 15 - 12 = 3, and the negative value has the larger absolute value.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q09::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L02_Q09
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Evaluate 7 - 11.
Movement reference: start at 7 and move 11 units left.
{
=A. -4#Correct. 7 - 11 means move 11 units left from 7, ending at -4.
~B. -18#This adds the absolute values and keeps a negative sign. The expression is subtraction.
~C. 4#This reverses the subtraction.
~D. 18#This adds the numbers instead of subtracting.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q10::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L02_Q10
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Evaluate 8 - (-5).
Movement reference: subtracting -5 means add 5, so move 5 units right from 8.
{
~A. 3#This treats subtracting a negative as ordinary subtraction.
~B. -13#This uses the correct distance but the wrong sign.
~C. -3#This subtracts and then uses a negative sign.
=D. 13#Correct. Subtracting a negative means adding the opposite: 8 + 5 = 13.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q11::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L02_Q11
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Evaluate -4 - 9.
Movement reference: start at -4 and move 9 more units left.
{
~A. -5#This subtracts the absolute values instead of moving left.
=B. -13#Correct. -4 - 9 means move 9 more units left from -4.
~C. 5#This ignores the negative direction.
~D. 13#This uses the right distance but the wrong sign.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q12::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L02_Q12
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Evaluate -3 - (-10).
Movement reference: subtracting -10 means add 10, so move 10 units right from -3.
{
~A. -13#This treats both values as negatives being added.
~B. -7#This uses the correct distance but wrong direction.
=C. 7#Correct. -3 - (-10) = -3 + 10 = 7.
~D. 13#This adds absolute values instead of adding the opposite.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q13::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L02_Q13
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Evaluate (-5)(6).
Sign-rule reference: different signs make a negative product.
{
=A. -30#Correct. Different signs make a negative product, and 5 times 6 = 30.
~B. -11#This adds the numbers instead of multiplying them.
~C. 30#This multiplies correctly but uses the wrong sign.
~D. 11#This adds absolute values and uses a positive sign.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q14::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L02_Q14
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Evaluate (-7)(-2).
Sign-rule reference: same signs make a positive product.
{
~A. -14#This multiplies correctly but uses the wrong sign. Same signs make a positive product.
~B. -9#This adds the values instead of multiplying.
=C. 14#Correct. A negative times a negative is positive, and 7 times 2 = 14.
~D. 9#This adds absolute values instead of multiplying.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q15::
